Basic Installation
==================

TCLAP is a header-only library, so on most modern systems you can
simply copy the .../include/tclap directory to your project, or simply
add .../include to the include path for the compiler. The path prefix
"..." here refers to the root directory of TCLAP (i.e., where you
cloned or extracted it).

CMake Installation
==================

TCLAP requires CMake 3.13 or later (https://cmake.org/) to configure,
build, test, and install the library. Run the following commands from
the root TCLAP directory:

cmake -S . -B build
cmake --build build
cmake --build build --target install

This detects the system configuration and installs the headers in the
default install location. To install in an alternative location, use:

cmake -S . -B build -DCMAKE_INSTALL_PREFIX=</your/install/prefix>
cmake --build build --target install

After TCLAP has been installed, you can use it by adding the install
location to your compilers include path and define HAVE_TCLAP_CONFIG_H
to use the config picked up by the system. On most systems the
defaults are good enough and it is not necessary to rely on the
configuration detected by CMake - it is mostly used to work around
some old compiler and Windows issues.

Building without tests
======================

The default project build includes examples, unit tests and portable fuzz
seed tests. The example tests require Python 3. Set BUILD_TESTING=OFF to
skip test registration and the Python dependency. Unit tests and portable
fuzz regression runners default to the initial value of BUILD_TESTING;
explicitly enabled fuzz targets can still be built without registering tests.
Use a fresh build directory when switching tests off: existing directories
can retain old CTest registrations and cached developer-target options.

For a library-only configuration, use a fresh build directory and configure
with BUILD_TESTING=OFF, BUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF and BUILD_DOC=OFF, for example
(from the build directory):

cmake -DBUILD_TESTING=OFF -DBUILD_EXAMPLES=OFF -DBUILD_DOC=OFF ..

VisualStudio Users
==================

VisualStudio can work with CMake, use the "File->Open Folder" option
to import the project into VisualStudio.

Release staging
---------------
Generate documentation with BUILD_DOC=ON, then stage it with:

  bash scripts/release.sh SOURCE_DIR DEST_DIR BUILD_DIR

BUILD_DIR is optional and defaults to SOURCE_DIR/build. Paths may contain spaces.
